1
00:00:00,290 --> 00:00:02,893
Your app stores its settings
in a Snowflake table.

2
00:00:02,958 --> 00:00:06,115
Test each change on a copy of the table,
called a clone.

3
00:00:06,180 --> 00:00:09,081
Have someone else approve it
before you update the original.

4
00:00:09,930 --> 00:00:13,535
In this demo, a fictional store
is testing a new checkout page.

5
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:16,608
Today, 25% of testers see that page.

6
00:00:16,673 --> 00:00:20,300
In the Developer app, propose changing
that setting to 50%.

7
00:00:21,130 --> 00:00:22,639
Run the update on the clone.

8
00:00:22,704 --> 00:00:27,184
The preview shows one row changing
from 25% to 50%.

9
00:00:27,249 --> 00:00:30,257
The original table still reads 25%.

10
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:32,669
Send the preview to a reviewer.

11
00:00:32,734 --> 00:00:35,998
In the Admin app, they test the change
again on current data.

12
00:00:36,063 --> 00:00:38,000
They check the result and approve it.

13
00:00:38,850 --> 00:00:40,537
The reviewer selects Execute.

14
00:00:40,602 --> 00:00:43,354
They check the table name
and confirm the update.

15
00:00:43,419 --> 00:00:46,192
The original table now reads 50%.

16
00:00:47,010 --> 00:00:50,679
The audit log shows who requested the change
and who approved it.

17
00:00:50,744 --> 00:00:54,286
Use the query ID to look up
the exact update that ran.
